WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.500
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.950
Today we're learning more about

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.710
the takings clause of the Fifth Amendment.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.210
In another video we'll discuss

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.020
the other clauses of the Fifth Amendment,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.260
those that deal with self-incrimination

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.700
and due process of law.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.270
But in this video we're concentrating

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.270
on just the last few words
of the Fifth Amendment

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.870
which forbid the government
from taking private property

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.290
for public use without just compensation.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.400
To learn more about the takings clause,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.520
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.160
Richard Epstein is the Lawrence
A Tisch Professor of Law

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.900
and Director of the Classical
Liberal Institute at NYU Law.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.620
He's also a Senior Fellow
at the Hoover Institution.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.400
Eduardo Penalver is the
Allan R. Tessler Professor

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.820
and Dean of Cornell Law School.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.590
So Prof. Epstein can you
give us a little background?

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.830
Just what is the taking clause?

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.200
- [Richard] Well, there's a
rule in Constitutional Law

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.530
that the shorter the provision,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.730
the more difficult the interpretation.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.380
And this is a very short petition.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.157
It says, "nor shall
private property be taken

00:01:02.157 --> 00:01:05.940
"for public use without
just compensation."

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.190
And the first thing to
understand about the clause

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.240
is that it's in the passive voice.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.450
So it doesn't tell you who's taking it.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.590
And early on, since this was
the part of the Bill of Rights,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.160
it was said to apply only to
Congress and not to the states.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.730
And then after the Civil War,
through the 14th Amendment,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.580
it was said to apply to the states.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.047
So now it applies to both the states

00:01:27.047 --> 00:01:29.030
and to the Federal government.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.210
- [Eduardo] Many constitutions don't have

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.180
an explicit property protection clause,

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.750
but there's a fairly strong norm

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.590
around compensating
owners of private property

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.790
when you, essentially
commandeer their property

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.880
for public purposes.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.380
And that seems to have
been the motivation.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.090
It's, the clearest reading of it is that

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:55.500
it's a provision of the
Constitution that makes clear

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.060
that when the Federal
government takes your property,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.630
or when the states takes your
property for some public use,

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.560
that they have to make you whole

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.030
by giving you just compensation.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.500
- [Richard] Private property's
a pretty comprehensive term.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.630
Everybody understands that it means land

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.190
and the things you build on land.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.000
They also understand that
it tends to cover chattels,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.960
that is things like books and baseballs

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.000
that you happen to own and wild animals.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.730
But it's also, it's
much broader than that.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.840
It covers all sorts of intangible rights,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.960
like patents, copyrights, trade secrets.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.750
These are very complicated
and generally speaking

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.430
the government doesn't have any obligation

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.870
to create a patent,

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.220
but once it gives you the patent

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.870
it just can't take it from you

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.020
because the patent after
conferred, is in fact,

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.730
now a property right.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.680
And then it also may or may not cover

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.300
other kinds of intangibles like good will

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.200
which is the value
associated with the business,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.880
knowing that your past customers

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.140
may come back to you in the future.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.810
- [Eduardo] On the motives of the Framers

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:01.210
for including this talk about
things like the confiscation,

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.120
mostly of personal property

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.723
during the Revolutionary War
by the British government,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.380
that was possibly motivation to make clear

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.820
that if in future scenarios

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.630
if the government wanted
to take your property

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:18.040
it would have to, it would
have to compensate you.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.560
And I think most historians don't think

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.580
there was a real problem
being addressed by this.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.240
A problem of uncompensated
taking of property

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.130
by colonial governments
during that period,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.280
or by the state governments

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.160
during the Articles of Confederation.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.580
- [Richard] The Framers wanted a system

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.870
of what we call limited government.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.227
And so the takings clause,
essentially says, is

00:03:40.227 --> 00:03:42.637
"yes, we need your land for a fort,

00:03:42.637 --> 00:03:44.647
"and it's really very important.

00:03:44.647 --> 00:03:46.887
"But if we're gonna take
it and use it for a fort,

00:03:46.887 --> 00:03:50.197
"that's a public use, we're
gonna pay you compensation

00:03:50.197 --> 00:03:51.957
"for the fair market value of the land

00:03:51.957 --> 00:03:53.880
"before the fort was put on."

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.770
And this is in order to make sure

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.960
that you can't pick and
choose your victims,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.430
and it's a way of assuring
government regularity.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.620
That's the first.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.760
Second thing is more economic,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.800
less apparent at the time of the Framing,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.880
but pretty apparent today.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.350
Is if the government can take something

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.550
and not pay you compensation,
it's gonna overtake.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.700
So your land is worth $100,000 as a farm,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.760
but if you're gonna use it for a fort

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.750
it's only worth $10,000.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:22.800
If you don't have to pay the $100,000,

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.250
well you may take it because
you get $10,000 worth of gain.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.460
But if you have to pay the
fellow a fair market value

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.950
of the property interest
taking, you won't do it.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.580
So essentially what it
does is it makes sure,

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.330
or at least improves the odds,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.120
that when the government
does take property,

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.480
or does regulate, it will in fact

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.440
improve overall social welfare.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.300
So you have a political function
dealing with singling out

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.210
and you have an economic function

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.240
dealing with the overall
improvement of government behavior

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.170
from an economic point of view.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.540
- [Kim] Okay, so say I had a piece of land

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:56.920
and the government decided

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.680
that they wanted it for some purpose.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.660
What would be the legal process

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.750
for the government to go
about acquiring my land?

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.100
- [Eduardo] Well, the
process for eminent domain

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.540
really varies by state,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.540
but typically there's some notice

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.130
that the government gives you

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.050
that it intends to take your property.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.730
The government goes to court

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.510
and gets an order of condemnation

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:19.990
to take title of the property.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.100
And then often as a requirement

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.360
that the government bargain with you

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.390
about the value of the property.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.680
It will tell you what
it thinks the value is,

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.970
the fair market value is
the standard that we use

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.180
for just compensation.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.270
That's the value that a
willing buyer would pay

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.150
to a willing seller for the land.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.620
It doesn't include things
like your sentimental value

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:43.453
or anything like that.

00:05:43.453 --> 00:05:46.250
So it's just the market
value of the property.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.550
There's some back and forth

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:49.600
through this required bargaining.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.580
If the government and the property owner

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.413
can't reach an agreement,

00:05:52.413 --> 00:05:53.770
then the government can go to court

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:57.520
and get the court to specify
the value of the property

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.200
that would aid in compensation.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.010
And then the payment is made
and the deed is transferred

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.710
and the government becomes
the owner of the property.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.370
- [Kim] Interesting, so the government

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:09.780
has gone through this process
to try to acquire my land.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.190
What if I'm a real hold-out

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.800
and I just really don't want
the government to get my land?

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:15.633
What happens then?

00:06:15.633 --> 00:06:17.997
- [Eduardo] Well, you can
litigate various pieces of this,

00:06:17.997 --> 00:06:20.210
and again it depends a
little bit on the state law,

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:23.390
so some states put more procedural hurdles

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:24.503
in the way of the government,

00:06:24.503 --> 00:06:27.660
then other states make it
easier for the government.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:31.250
But under the Constitution
there are really only two ways

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.120
you can resist the taking of your property

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:34.953
through eminent domain.

00:06:34.953 --> 00:06:38.770
One is by arguing that the
use that the government plans

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.450
to make of your property doesn't count

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.690
as a legitimate public use.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.077
Because the clause says,
"nor shall private property

00:06:45.077 --> 00:06:47.650
"be taken for public use
without just compensation."

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.990
And that public use language
has been interpreted

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.780
to be a limitation on the
power of eminent domain.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.790
And then you can argue, the
second thing you can argue

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.750
is that what the
government is offering you

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.453
in terms of just compensation
is not adequate compensation.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.400
And you can litigate
those through the courts

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.300
and that can slow the
process down quite a bit.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.267
Some states make it easier
for the government by saying,

00:07:08.267 --> 00:07:10.737
"well the government can
actually just take title,

00:07:10.737 --> 00:07:12.320
"while you litigate."

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.070
And others make you go, you know,

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.330
allow you to stop the condemnation process

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.630
and litigate in advance.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:25.420
But there's often what's
called a quick-take procedure

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.863
which allows the government
to move more quickly

00:07:27.863 --> 00:07:29.910
and put the litigation on the back end.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:34.350
But the public use, if the
use is found not to be public,

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.230
what you win is, you got
an actual prohibition

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.910
on the taking itself.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:39.840
You keep your property.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.160
If you win on the just compensation side,

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.623
all you get is more money,
they still take your property.

00:07:44.623 --> 00:07:45.456
(laughs)

00:07:45.456 --> 00:07:47.400
- [Kim] So, you mentioned public use.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.880
What counts as public use?

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:52.500
- [Eduardo] Well, under the current law,

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:55.400
any use that generates a public benefit

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.740
is really a public use.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:00.920
So the way the court has put
it is anything the government,

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.040
any purpose the government can pursue

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.580
through any other means,

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.930
it can pursue through eminent domain.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.330
So, if the government
wants to create jobs,

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.050
or if the government wants to beautify,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:18.387
or if the government
wants to remove blight,

00:08:18.387 --> 00:08:21.100
all those things are things
that we think it's legitimate

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.030
for the government to try to do.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.530
If it can do those things, it can do them

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.630
through the use of the
eminent domain power.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.270
- [Kim] One thing that
strikes me as interesting

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:35.270
is just the fact that this
clause is in the Fifth Amendment

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:40.460
among things like, double
jeopardy or self-incrimination.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:42.940
So why do you think the Framers included

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.300
this particular clause here

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.030
as opposed to elsewhere
in the Bill of Rights?

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.390
- [Richard] Well that's a great question

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:50.950
to which there's no obvious answer,

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.980
but the one clause that
is pretty close to it

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.370
is the due process clause
in the Fifth Amendment

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.797
which says, "nor shall
any person be deprived

00:08:58.797 --> 00:09:01.950
"of life, liberty or property
without due process of law."

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.110
So if you start with the procedural stuff,

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:07.097
somebody's gonna say,
"well, how do we know

00:09:07.097 --> 00:09:09.340
"that the procedures aren't fair?"

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.500
And if it turns out
that the government uses

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.040
a set of procedures that don't
give you adequate notice,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.740
you're always going to end up short

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.550
on the amount of compensation
that you're gonna acquire.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:22.710
So bad procedures tend
to lead to bad outcomes

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.080
which tend to lead to property being taken

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.180
at less than full market value.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.120
So what happens is is
therefore very close linkage

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.150
between the procedures used
under the due process clause

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:37.400
and the substance of protection
that you have under takings.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:40.010
- [Kim] Any changes in the interpretation

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.770
of the takings clause by
the Supreme Court over time?

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.470
- [Richard] Well there's been a huge

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.050
kind of switch up and down

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:48.550
and let me put it the following way.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.950
In the beginning there
wasn't much of anything

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:53.480
that was done with respect
to the takings clause.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.800
The first Federal case to deal with it

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.770
was called Barron against
Baltimore in 1833 or so.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.590
And it just simply said that
the clause does not allow

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:08.630
for protection of Mr. Barron
against the city of Baltimore,

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.940
because it only binds
the Federal government,

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.550
it doesn't bind the state.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:15.690
After the Civil War, two things happened

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.480
of real distinction.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:18.390
The first thing is

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.200
you start getting comprehensive regulation

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.000
of the railroads and the
rates they can charge

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.820
and then public utilities

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:27.650
and the rates that they can charge.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:31.090
And then in 1921 there's a
case called Block v. Hirsch

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.770
and it's a very close
five to four decision.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.580
But they sustain on the
grounds that it's a war time,

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:39.420
a temporary two year statue
that limits the rents

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.800
that can be charged in Washington D.C.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:42.633
at the end of the first World War.

00:10:42.633 --> 00:10:44.140
- [Eduardo] One of the biggest changes

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:45.640
in the interpretation
of the takings clause

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.350
was the extension of the
takings clause to govern

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.330
situations in which your property

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.210
was not appropriated by the government.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.860
So what we called the
Regulatory Takings Doctrine

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.540
which started in, with the case

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:04.480
of Pennsylvania Coal
versus Mahon in the 1920s

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.000
and then has now become much more,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.380
it was sort of dormant for a while,

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.193
would become much more
active since the 1980s.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.070
That's a doctrine that says
if the government regulates

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.840
your use of property excessively,

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.280
then the courts can treat that,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.810
in effect as a confiscation
of your property

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.860
and require the payment
of just compensation.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:27.440
That was a pretty, that's maybe
the most significant change

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.030
in the interpretation of
the clause in its history.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:32.680
So there continues to be controversy

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.410
about the public use doctrine.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:37.010
Modern commentators,
especially Libertarians

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:40.360
want a very narrowly drawn
account of public use

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:43.460
and disagree with the
breadth of the doctrine

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.580
as I described it to you.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.710
And so there's a lot of
activism around that.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:49.980
There's been some state
laws that's been enacted

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.220
to try to raise the floor there.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.150
The Federal definition of
public use is just a floor

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:56.660
and states can go beyond that

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:58.910
and restrict the power of eminent domain

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:00.410
more forcefully if they want to.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:01.810
And many have.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.720
- [Kim] So we've learned
that the takings clause

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:04.710
of the Fifth Amendment

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.160
prevents both Federal
and state governments

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.900
from taking private
property for public use

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.720
without just compensation.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.780
But there are a lot of
questions about what counts

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.810
as private property, public
use or just compensation.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.240
In recent years debate
over the takings clause

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.380
has centered on whether
government regulations

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.040
about how a private
individual can use land

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.500
also constitute a form of confiscation.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.630
And the extent of acceptable public uses

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:33.870
for which the government
can seize property.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.650
To learn more about the Fifth Amendment,

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:37.630
visit the National Constitution Centers

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.530
interactive constitution
and Khan Academy's resources

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.353
on U.S. government and politics.

